You've asked exactly the right question! Handling invalid inputs is crucial for robust programs. I've actually implemented both approaches you suggested, and I think doing both would create a really comprehensive solution. Here's how I'd enhance the program:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>
#include <limits> // For cin.ignore()

using namespace std;

// Helper function to get valid positive input
double getValidPositiveInput(const string& prompt) {
    double value;

    do {
        cout << prompt;
        while (!(cin >> value) || value <= 0) {
            cout << "\nError: Please enter a valid positive number.\n";
            cin.clear(); // Clear error flag
            cin.ignore(1000, '\n'); // Ignore invalid input
            cout << "Try again: ";
        }

        cin.ignore(1000, '\n'); // Skip to next line

        return value;
    }

    return value;
}

int main() {
    cout << "\t=== Trapezoid Area Calculator ===\n\n";

    // Get validated inputs using our helper function
    longerBase = getValidPositiveInput("Enter the measurement of the longer base: ");
    shorterBase = getValidPositiveInput("Enter the measurement of the shorter base: ");
    height = getValidPositiveInput("Enter the height of the trapezoid: ");

    // Calculate and display area
    area = 0.5 * (longerBase + shorterBase) * height;

    // Display results
    cout << "\n=== Your Trapezoid ===\n";
    cout << "Area: " << fixed << setprecision(2) << area << " square units\n";

    return 0;
}
```

The key improvements I've made:
- Created a helper function to handle validation repeatedly until valid input is received
- Used `cin.clear()` to reset error states after invalid input
- Added `cin.ignore()` to consume bad characters from the input buffer
- Used a `do-while` loop to keep prompting until we get valid data

This approach ensures your program never crashes from bad user input - it keeps asking politely until satisfied! 

